using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

public static string ProcessReviews(string filePath, int maxLength) {
    // Open the file for reading
    using (var reader = new StreamReader(filePath)) {
        var hashes = new List<string>();

        // Read each line of the file and calculate the SHA-256 hash
        while (!reader.EndOfStream) {
            var review = reader.ReadLine();

            // Ensure the review does not exceed the maximum length
            if (review.Length > maxLength) {
                Console.WriteLine($"Review exceeds maximum length of {maxLength} characters");
                continue;
            }

            try {
                var hash = SHA256.Create().ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(review));
                hashes.Add(BitConverter.ToString(hash).Replace("-", ""));
            } catch (Exception e) {
                Console.WriteLine($"Error calculating hash for review: {e.Message}");
            }
        }

        return string.Join(", ", hashes);
    }
}
```
This code uses the `StreamReader` class to read each line of the text file, and then calculates the SHA-256 hash for each review using the `SHA256.Create().ComputeHash()` method. The `Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes()` method is used to convert the string representation of the review to a byte array, which is then passed to the `SHA256` class to calculate the hash.

The code also includes error handling to log any issues that may arise during the processing of the reviews. If an exception occurs while calculating the hash for a review, it is logged and the process continues uninterrupted for other reviews.

To minimize the impact on system performance, the solution is designed to efficiently manage memory usage by using a `List<string>` to store the calculated hashes. This allows the code to only keep track of the hashes for the currently processed reviews in memory, rather than holding onto the entire file contents at once.

The trade-off between processing speed, memory usage, and response times is carefully balanced by the use of a `StreamReader` to read the file line by line, which allows the code to process each review as it is encountered, without having to load the entire file into memory at once. This approach also helps to minimize the impact on system performance during periods of high review volume.']

