### Structs and Enums

Structs are similar to classes in other programming languages. They are used to group data elements together into a single unit, allowing for more efficient use of memory and code organization. Enums are used to define a set of named constants that can be used in the program.

```c
// struct definition
struct point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

// struct instance creation and usage
struct point p1 = { 3, 4 };
printf("%d\n", p1.x); // prints "3"

// enum definition
enum color { RED, GREEN, BLUE };

// enum usage
enum color c1 = RED;
printf("%d\n", c1); // prints "0"
```

### Pointers

Pointers are variables that store memory addresses as values. They are used to manipulate memory directly and can be used to create complex data structures, implement dynamic memory allocation, and access elements of an array or a string using a pointer arithmetic expression.

```c
// declare and initialize a pointer variable
int* p;
p = &a;

// access the value pointed by the pointer
printf("%d\n", *p); // prints "12"

// modify the value pointed by the pointer
*p = 10;
```

### Arrays and Strings

Arrays are collections of elements that share a common data type. They are used to store multiple values in a single block of memory, allowing for faster access and manipulation of the elements. Strings are arrays of characters, representing textual data.

```c
// declare and initialize an array variable
int arr[3] = { 1, 2, 3 };

// access elements of the array
printf("%d\n", arr[0]); // prints "1"
printf("%d\n", arr[2]); // prints "3"

// modify elements of the array
arr[0] = 4;

// declare and initialize a string variable
char str[] = "Hello, World!";

// access characters in the string
printf("%c\n", str[0]); // prints "H"
```

### Functions

Functions are blocks of code that can be called multiple times from different parts of the program. They are used to perform specific tasks and allow for code reuse. Functions can take arguments, which are values passed into the function when it is called, and return values, which are produced by the function.

```c
// define a function with no arguments and no return value
void greet(void) {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");
}

// call the function
greet(); // prints "Hello, World!"
```']

