The `nfapi_rrc_cco_request_list_init` function initializes a new list with the specified capacity. It allocates an array of pointers to NFAPI_RRC_CCO_REQUEST structures and sets the count to 0.

The `nfapi_rrc_cco_request_list_add` function adds a new NFAPI_RRC_CCO_REQUEST structure to the list. It first acquires a lock on the list using pthread mutex, then checks if the list is at capacity. If it is, it resizes the list by doubling its capacity and reallocates memory for the updated array of pointers. Finally, it adds the new request to the end of the list and increments the count.

The `nfapi_rrc_cco_request_list_remove` function removes an NFAPI_RRC_CCO_REQUEST structure from the list based on a specific criteria (e.g., UE_ID). It first acquires a lock on the list, then searches for the specified request in the list using a linear search. If it is found, it removes the request from the list by shifting all subsequent requests one position to the left. Finally, it decrements the count and releases the lock.

The `nfapi_rrc_cco_request_list_filter` function filters the list based on a specific criteria (e.g., UE_ID or priority level). It first acquires a lock on the list, then searches for the specified request in the list using a linear search. If it is found, it removes the request from the list by shifting all subsequent requests one position to the left. Finally, it releases the lock and returns the updated list.

The `nfapi_rrc_cco_request_list_get` function gets an NFAPI_RRC_CCO_REQUEST structure with a specific UE_ID from the list. It first acquires a lock on the list, then searches for the specified request in the list using a linear search. If it is found, it returns the request and releases the lock.

The `nfapi_rrc_cco_request_list_free` function frees the memory allocated for the list and its elements. It first acquires a lock on the list, then destroys the mutex and frees the memory for the array of pointers to NFAPI_RRC_CCO_REQUEST structures. Finally, it releases the lock.']

