    pool->pool_array[idx].rnti = rnti;
    pool->pool_array[idx].crc_indication_rel8_tag = rand() % 2 == 0 ? CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG_1 : CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG_2;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&pool->mutex); // Unlock mutex
}

// Get a random element from the pool
int get_element(struct pool *pool) {
    if (pool->pool_size == 0) { // No elements in the pool?
        return -1;
    }
    int idx = rand() % pool->pool_size; // Randomly select an element from the pool
    return pool->pool_array[idx].rx_ue_information_tag;
}

// Free the entire pool
void free_pool(struct pool *pool) {
    if (pool == NULL) { // No pool to free?
        return;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < pool->pool_size; ++i) { // Release each element in the pool
        free(pool->pool_array[i].pdu.rx_ue_information);
        free(pool->pool_array[i].pdu.rnti);
    }
    free(pool->pool_array); // Release the array of pool elements
    pool = NULL;
}
```
This implementation includes a `struct pool` data structure that manages an array of `pool_element_t` structures, each containing a PDU, RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG, RNTI, and CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG. The `init_pool()` function initializes the pool with a given initial size, allocating memory for the array of pool elements. The `add_element()` function adds a new element to the pool by reallocating the memory if necessary (i.e., when the pool size reaches its maximum), and then randomly sets the CRCFLAG in the CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG field. The `get_element()` function gets a random element from the pool, returning -1 if there are no elements in the pool. Finally, the `free_pool()` function frees the entire pool by releasing each element in the pool and then the array of pool elements itself.

The implementation also includes mechanisms to prevent memory leaks or excessive CPU usage during high-throughput operations:

