    for (int i = 0; i < num_network_interfaces; i++) {
        if (strcmp(deleted_iface->name, network_interfaces[i].name) == 0) {
            index = i;
            break;
        }
    }

    // If the interface is found, delete it from the array
    if (index != -1) {
        memmove(&network_interfaces[index], &network_interfaces[index + 1], sizeof(struct network_interface) * (num_network_interfaces - index - 1));
        num_network_interfaces--;
    } else {
        printf("Network interface %s not found\n", deleted_iface->name);
    }

    // Release the mutex
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&network_interfaces_lock);
}
```
This function provides an API for adding, updating, and deleting network interfaces from a list of structures. The `add_network_interface` function takes a pointer to a `struct network_interface` as input and adds it to the end of the array. The `update_network_interface` function takes a pointer to a `struct network_interface` as input and updates the configuration of an existing interface in the list. The `delete_network_interface` function takes a pointer to a `struct network_interface` as input and deletes it from the list if it is found.

The implementation uses a mutex to protect access to the `network_interfaces` array from multiple threads, ensuring that concurrent updates are handled correctly. The function also includes error handling mechanisms for missing interfaces or other edge cases.

This implementation should meet the performance constraints of the system by minimizing memory usage and providing quick response times under high-load conditions. It also provides a robust and scalable architecture that can adapt to future increases in interface count without significant changes to the codebase. The usability of the function is ensured through a clear and efficient API, making it easy for other modules to interact with the network configuration data.']

